// Lafayette Interior Fashions is named here and only here (rule 8 reserves
// the name for this page; Home and the services overview use "the lines I
// represent"). No logo or trademark is used, per rule 8.
//
// Naming Lafayette as the source is what rule 7 and 8.3 ask for. A formal
// relationship claim is not: the page said "Amy is a Lafayette Interior
// Fashions dealer" until 2026-07-26, which is an unconfirmed factual claim
// about a business relationship (BUILD-PLAN decision 12). Softened to "the one
// line I represent in this category," matching the holding pattern Stout and
// Pollack are already under. Restore the dealer wording only once Amy
// confirms it.
//
// Keyword cluster (8.3): "custom blinds Cincinnati, custom shades Northern
// Kentucky, Lafayette blinds Cincinnati" — title, H1, first paragraph, meta
// description.
//
// Roman shades appear as a searchable term but route to manufactured
// product, not Amy's workroom (5.3's own instruction) — the copy below says
// so explicitly rather than leaving it ambiguous.

import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import RevealImage from '../../components/RevealImage.astro';
import Accordion from '../../components/Accordion.astro';
import leadImage from '../../assets/images/portfolio/collected-living-room/banded-roman-shade-drapery-french-doors-detail.jpg';

const decisionCategories = [
  {
    title: 'Light and glare',
    content:
      'How much light a room should keep is a different question from how much it lets in. A west-facing room with a television needs glare handled at one specific hour. A kitchen usually wants every bit of daylight it can hold. Solar shades are rated by openness, and that number decides how much of your view survives when they are down.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Privacy',
    content:
      'Privacy and light are not the same requirement, and treating them as one is the most common mistake here. A top-down bottom-up shade covers the lower half of the window and leaves the daylight coming in above it, which is usually exactly what a street-facing bedroom or bathroom wants. Sheers give you daytime privacy and then read completely differently after dark, once the lamps are on inside.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Motorization',
    content:
      'Motorization earns its keep on tall windows, wide spans, and anything stranded above a stair or behind furniture, the treatments nobody ever adjusts because reaching them is a nuisance. It also lets a whole room drop on a schedule, ahead of the afternoon sun instead of after the room has already cooked. It is far easier to decide before drywall than after.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Children and pets',
    content:
      'Cordless, in any house with young children or pets. Cordless lift, motorization, and tensioned continuous-loop mounts all take the hazard of a pull cord off the table. Worth deciding on purpose rather than by omission.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Room by room',
    content:
      'Bathrooms and kitchens need something that shrugs off moisture and wipes clean. Bedrooms usually want room-darkening or blackout, often layered under drapery. Living rooms need something adjustable enough for a whole day of moving light. Woven woods warm up a room that reads flat. Shutters suit a window where the treatment should read as architecture rather than fabric.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Custom Blinds and Shades Cincinnati | Amy K Clark Design"
  description="Custom blinds Cincinnati and custom shades Northern Kentucky. Lafayette blinds, shades, shutters, and woven woods, measured and installed."
  ogImage="/images/og/og-default.jpg"
  transparentNav={false}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { name: 'Services', href: '/services' },
    { name: 'Blinds and Shades', href: '/services/blinds-shades' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="section wrap service-page__intro">
    <p class="type-label service-page__eyebrow">Services</p>
    <h1 class="type-display service-page__heading">Blinds and Shades</h1>
    <p class="service-page__lead">
      Blinds and shades for Cincinnati and Northern Kentucky homes, specified for the way you
      actually use the room. Lafayette Interior Fashions is the one line I represent here, which
      means blinds, shades, shutters, and woven woods all come from a single catalog I know cold.
    </p>
    <p class="service-page__lead">
      Roman shades come from Lafayette as a manufactured product. If your room wants a fabric roman
      made to order, a particular textile with a particular band, that is workroom work and I
      handle it as custom drapery instead.
    </p>
  </section>

  <RevealImage
    src={leadImage}
    alt="Banded roman shade and pleated drapery framing French doors, with a crystal chandelier overhead"
    blurDataURL="data:image/webp;base64,UklGRqwAAABXRUJQVlA4IKAAAAAwBQCdASoYABUAPu1orlCppaQiqAqpMB2JZwAG0KCFeA8GvwPjvP6bHUT+YIQzyU7j0AD+FSHDV0ubeTAPHbf5413BYJW3pLc2ZHNK0MCBuh5vMwaNlvdw15DG9e1iBJcqwHR2FKUtXrkLqItBnK/DhKwBxjlP+OnZmgp6tjAHPHdy98FzAZDZkNeAJofae+wgRZ+DQT6E0ZUr/QTv/QAA"
    aspectRatio="16/9"
    sizes="100vw"
  />

  <section class="section wrap service-page__body">
    <h2 class="type-display service-page__subhead">Decide these before anything is ordered</h2>
    <p>
      Most regret about blinds and shades comes from a decision nobody actually made. A shade
      picked for how it looked in a showroom, not for what your window is doing at four in the
      afternoon.
    </p>

    <Accordion items={decisionCategories} />

    <h2 class="type-display service-page__subhead">An eighth of an inch</h2>
    <p>
      Blinds and shades are unforgiving about measurement in a way drapery is not. An eighth of an
      inch decides whether an inside mount clears the frame or binds against it. I measure every
      opening myself and install the finished product myself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Windows in older Cincinnati and Northern Kentucky houses are almost never square. Allowing
      for that correctly is what keeps a treatment from looking almost right.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap contact-band bg-ground">
    <p class="type-display contact-band__line">Ready to start your project?</p>
    <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-solid">Schedule a Consultation</a>
  </section>
</Base>
